1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,920
 - So now we're going to write a search engine,

2
00:00:02,920 --> 00:00:04,920
 do some of the things, we're going to do page rank

3
00:00:04,920 --> 00:00:07,700
 and we're going to visualize it in a web browser

4
00:00:07,700 --> 00:00:09,060
 and show the weights.

5
00:00:09,060 --> 00:00:11,520
 We're really only going to do page rank on one page

6
00:00:11,520 --> 00:00:14,420
 because you want to have links that more than one page

7
00:00:14,420 --> 00:00:16,780
 that points to a page so that you can figure out

8
00:00:16,780 --> 00:00:19,080
 which pages are more or less important.

9
00:00:19,080 --> 00:00:20,080
 And then we visualize it.

10
00:00:20,080 --> 00:00:21,320
 We'll run the page rank algorithm

11
00:00:21,320 --> 00:00:23,220
 and we'll separately do all this.

12
00:00:23,220 --> 00:00:26,100
 So at this point we're going to do pretty much the web crawling,

13
00:00:26,100 --> 00:00:27,420
 the index building, and the searching,

14
00:00:27,420 --> 00:00:28,460
 we're not going to really search it,

15
00:00:28,460 --> 00:00:30,220
 we're going to visualize the index.

16
00:00:30,220 --> 00:00:32,540
 But you could write a simple program

17
00:00:32,540 --> 00:00:34,860
 to do searches for keywords and figure out which page

18
00:00:34,860 --> 00:00:36,620
 was the most likely page for a keyword.

19
00:00:36,620 --> 00:00:39,980
 And that would be a fun additional thing to do.

20
00:00:39,980 --> 00:00:43,500
 So the web crawler is this program that hits a page,

21
00:00:43,500 --> 00:00:46,620
 pulls down the HTML, parses the page, looks for links,

22
00:00:46,620 --> 00:00:48,520
 makes a queue of incoming links

23
00:00:48,520 --> 00:00:50,300
 that are as yet unretrieved.

24
00:00:50,300 --> 00:00:53,500
 And I'm going to do this in a simple SQLite database

25
00:00:53,500 --> 00:00:56,100
 and starts out with, the database basically starts

26
00:00:56,100 --> 00:00:57,980
 with one link as the starting point

27
00:00:57,980 --> 00:00:59,220
 and then it retrieves that page

28
00:00:59,220 --> 00:01:01,460
 and then you see the database end up with

29
00:01:01,460 --> 00:01:03,660
 lots of unretrieved pages and then it goes back in

30
00:01:03,660 --> 00:01:05,660
 and picks a random page and retrieves that one

31
00:01:05,660 --> 00:01:08,420
 and then it just expands and expands.

32
00:01:08,420 --> 00:01:10,660
 This code that I've built that you're going to play with

33
00:01:10,660 --> 00:01:14,020
 is only stays on one website, otherwise it would go crazy.

34
00:01:14,020 --> 00:01:16,380
 And of course Google doesn't use an SQLite database

35
00:01:16,380 --> 00:01:19,060
 running on your hard drive, but you'll get the idea.

36
00:01:19,060 --> 00:01:23,340
 You'll see this thing exponentially gain links.

37
00:01:23,340 --> 00:01:24,700
 And you'll run it for a while,

38
00:01:24,700 --> 00:01:27,460
 pull down 1,000 web pages or whatever.

39
00:01:27,460 --> 00:01:31,660
 But of course, make sure that you don't violate

40
00:01:31,660 --> 00:01:33,180
 any terms, conditions, and again,

41
00:01:33,180 --> 00:01:36,860
 I've got some data sources that you can use

42
00:01:36,860 --> 00:01:38,780
 and they're not rate limited,

43
00:01:38,780 --> 00:01:41,220
 but you can also use things like Wikipedia,

44
00:01:41,220 --> 00:01:43,100
 which I think they sort of discourage you,

45
00:01:43,100 --> 00:01:45,260
 or drchuck.com, which has no rate limit,

46
00:01:45,260 --> 00:01:47,340
 or who knows what, right?

47
00:01:47,340 --> 00:01:49,980
 So just be careful, don't do this on Facebook

48
00:01:49,980 --> 00:01:52,820
 and don't do it on Google, don't get yourself in trouble.

49
00:01:52,820 --> 00:01:57,820
 And if you're using an internet connection

50
00:01:57,820 --> 00:02:01,060
 where you're paying for bandwidth, be careful.

51
00:02:01,060 --> 00:02:02,940
 So this is the idea of the web crawler,

52
00:02:02,940 --> 00:02:03,980
 and this isn't my picture,

53
00:02:03,980 --> 00:02:06,380
 this is the classic picture of a web crawler.

54
00:02:06,380 --> 00:02:10,140
 Read a page, parse it, take all the URLs

55
00:02:10,140 --> 00:02:13,700
 and stick them in a queue, grab again and again.

56
00:02:13,700 --> 00:02:16,100
 So for us, the scheduler is going to do it as long,

57
00:02:16,100 --> 00:02:18,060
 you'd say, oh, do 100 pages,

58
00:02:18,060 --> 00:02:19,380
 or it runs until it blows up.

59
00:02:19,380 --> 00:02:21,620
 I mean, and again, these processes

60
00:02:21,620 --> 00:02:23,380
 that have the network in the loop,

61
00:02:23,380 --> 00:02:25,860
 it's really important that they behave well

62
00:02:25,860 --> 00:02:26,700
 when they blow up.

63
00:02:26,700 --> 00:02:28,220
 And that's why databases are so useful,

64
00:02:28,220 --> 00:02:30,020
 because you can be writing along to the database

65
00:02:30,020 --> 00:02:34,400
 and some random thing happens and blows your data up

66
00:02:34,400 --> 00:02:35,240
 and you start over.

67
00:02:35,240 --> 00:02:37,980
 So you're reading these things, you're storing each page,

68
00:02:37,980 --> 00:02:40,100
 building up your storage, et cetera, et cetera.

69
00:02:40,100 --> 00:02:41,840
 So you just keep on doing that.

70
00:02:41,840 --> 00:02:44,900
 And with this program, you'll be able to retrieve some stuff,

71
00:02:44,900 --> 00:02:46,860
 then run the page rank, then you can retrieve them more,

72
00:02:46,860 --> 00:02:48,160
 and then you can run some more page rank,

73
00:02:48,160 --> 00:02:51,820
 and you can kind of see how Google sort of evolves

74
00:02:51,820 --> 00:02:53,220
 its index over time.

75
00:02:53,220 --> 00:02:55,380
 Of course, we're so much simpler.

76
00:02:55,380 --> 00:02:59,180
 And like I said, be careful when you crawl.

77
00:02:59,180 --> 00:03:03,060
 You're going to run a crawler that just goes as fast as it can,

78
00:03:03,060 --> 00:03:04,140
 but Google doesn't do that.

79
00:03:04,140 --> 00:03:07,060
 It's careful not to overwhelm any websites.

80
00:03:07,060 --> 00:03:09,860
 It's trying to be smart about the use of your bandwidth

81
00:03:09,860 --> 00:03:11,020
 on your website.

82
00:03:11,020 --> 00:03:13,620
 There is a file.

83
00:03:13,620 --> 00:03:16,460
 Our code won't bother looking at this.

84
00:03:18,980 --> 00:03:20,900
 But there's a file called robots.txt

85
00:03:20,900 --> 00:03:22,460
 that real web crawlers look at,

86
00:03:22,460 --> 00:03:23,980
 and it gives a list of the things

87
00:03:23,980 --> 00:03:26,900
 you are allowed to look at and not allowed to look at.

88
00:03:26,900 --> 00:03:29,340
 And so if you go to Google and you see a search that says,

89
00:03:29,340 --> 00:03:33,020
 we are not allowed to show you the summary text of this page

90
00:03:33,020 --> 00:03:35,660
 because of the robots.txt, it's there.

91
00:03:35,660 --> 00:03:40,220
 And you can go and you can actually see a robots.txt,

92
00:03:40,220 --> 00:03:43,820
 like on, just go to any website, it's at the top root,

93
00:03:43,820 --> 00:03:46,940
 blah, blah, blah, blah, blah, slash robots.txt.

94
00:03:46,940 --> 00:03:49,260
 It's not a path, it's not slash this, slash that,

95
00:03:49,260 --> 00:03:50,820
 slash something else robots.

96
00:03:50,820 --> 00:03:53,300
 It's at the very, very top of a website.

97
00:03:53,300 --> 00:03:56,900
 The index building uses the PageRank algorithm.

98
00:03:56,900 --> 00:03:58,740
 And the whole goal of the PageRank algorithm

99
00:03:58,740 --> 00:04:03,740
 is to figure out which pages have the most best links.

100
00:04:03,740 --> 00:04:08,700
 So having the most links is really easy.

101
00:04:08,700 --> 00:04:10,500
 You can just say how many links go to this.

102
00:04:10,500 --> 00:04:12,140
 But the problem is, is you got to figure out

103
00:04:12,140 --> 00:04:13,460
 the value of those links.

104
00:04:13,460 --> 00:04:15,100
 And then you have to, how do you figure the value

105
00:04:15,100 --> 00:04:20,100
 of those links by looking at how many good links come to it.

106
00:04:20,100 --> 00:04:23,060
 So it turns out that it's an infinite problem.

107
00:04:23,060 --> 00:04:27,140
 It's an infinitely difficult problem to use PageRank.

108
00:04:27,140 --> 00:04:29,820
 But you can approximate it, and what happens is,

109
00:04:29,820 --> 00:04:34,260
 after a while, it converges to a reasonable value.

110
00:04:34,260 --> 00:04:36,300
 And so we're going to run the search index,

111
00:04:36,300 --> 00:04:39,460
 and each time it runs, you're going to see that it says,

112
00:04:39,460 --> 00:04:41,180
 how much did these numbers change?

113
00:04:41,180 --> 00:04:42,940
 And what happens is, in the beginning,

114
00:04:42,940 --> 00:04:46,140
 they change very wildly, but quickly they flatten out.

115
00:04:46,140 --> 00:04:50,940
 And it has, the best way to think about the PageRank

116
00:04:50,940 --> 00:04:56,180
 is think about how water runs,

117
00:04:56,180 --> 00:05:01,180
 where you have a small little stream going by a house,

118
00:05:01,180 --> 00:05:05,420
 and sometimes it rains, sometimes it's dry,

119
00:05:05,420 --> 00:05:10,420
 and sometimes, and there's like a little lake,

120
00:05:10,420 --> 00:05:12,700
 and the stream is always running.

121
00:05:12,700 --> 00:05:14,420
 And it doesn't go up, and it doesn't go down.

122
00:05:14,420 --> 00:05:16,220
 It might go up a little bit if it rains a lot,

123
00:05:16,220 --> 00:05:18,860
 but in general, they're sort of a steady state,

124
00:05:18,860 --> 00:05:20,940
 meaning that whatever water's coming in

125
00:05:20,940 --> 00:05:23,640
 is about the same as the water going out.

126
00:05:23,640 --> 00:05:26,220
 So we think about this in terms of webpages.

127
00:05:26,220 --> 00:05:30,020
 The value of the links coming in is roughly the same

128
00:05:30,020 --> 00:05:31,380
 as the value of links going out.

129
00:05:31,380 --> 00:05:35,060
 So when that starts to balance the in and the out value

130
00:05:35,060 --> 00:05:38,940
 from each of the nodes, then you've got a pretty stable.

131
00:05:38,940 --> 00:05:41,620
 And so what Google does is they have a really

132
00:05:41,620 --> 00:05:45,460
 relatively stable assessment of goodness and value of pages,

133
00:05:45,460 --> 00:05:47,220
 and they use that to commute PageRank,

134
00:05:47,220 --> 00:05:48,820
 and then they throw a few more pages in,

135
00:05:48,820 --> 00:05:50,740
 and it kind of has to adjust for a while,

136
00:05:50,740 --> 00:05:51,660
 but it reconverges.

137
00:05:51,660 --> 00:05:55,800
 And so this is a calculation that generally converges,

138
00:05:55,800 --> 00:05:58,100
 and it doesn't vary wildly.

139
00:05:58,100 --> 00:06:01,940
 And that's why Google's pretty good at kind of arriving

140
00:06:01,940 --> 00:06:03,420
 at the true value of something.

141
00:06:03,420 --> 00:06:05,660
 So let's take a look at what we're going to do

142
00:06:05,660 --> 00:06:07,440
 in this application.

143
00:06:07,440 --> 00:06:12,440
 Again, we have a file that is going to Spider the Web,

144
00:06:12,440 --> 00:06:15,340
 and we only have one database.

145
00:06:15,340 --> 00:06:17,100
 Again, in this one, we'll have two databases.

146
00:06:17,100 --> 00:06:20,700
 In the next one, and so this is Spider,

147
00:06:20,700 --> 00:06:22,400
 is the restartable part.

148
00:06:22,400 --> 00:06:25,460
 And what we actually do is we put one URL in,

149
00:06:25,460 --> 00:06:29,660
 the starting URL, and then Spider walks in and asks,

150
00:06:29,660 --> 00:06:31,660
 are there any unretrieved pages?

151
00:06:31,660 --> 00:06:33,040
 And it does that randomly.

152
00:06:33,040 --> 00:06:35,340
 It sort of picks among the unretrieved pages and says,

153
00:06:35,340 --> 00:06:37,740
 okay, great, I'll go retrieve that page,

154
00:06:37,740 --> 00:06:39,100
 and then I'll parse that page,

155
00:06:39,100 --> 00:06:42,940
 and then I'll put in a bunch of new unretrieved pages,

156
00:06:42,940 --> 00:06:44,520
 as well as the text of that page

157
00:06:44,520 --> 00:06:46,820
 and then a bunch of unretrieved pages.

158
00:06:46,820 --> 00:06:48,980
 And then it'll go back up and it'll say,

159
00:06:48,980 --> 00:06:51,460
 oh, give me one of the randomly non-retrieved pages,

160
00:06:51,460 --> 00:06:53,580
 and I'll grab the next page and pull that page down,

161
00:06:53,580 --> 00:06:54,700
 and then add to it.

162
00:06:54,700 --> 00:06:58,300
 And so this is like, there's a page and then a to-do list,

163
00:06:58,300 --> 00:06:59,900
 and then this one becomes a page,

164
00:06:59,900 --> 00:07:01,940
 and then adds a few more things to the to-do list.

165
00:07:01,940 --> 00:07:04,660
 And so the to-do list, or the unretrieved URLs,

166
00:07:04,660 --> 00:07:06,240
 grows very rapidly.

167
00:07:06,240 --> 00:07:10,400
 And the retrieved ones grow sort of as,

168
00:07:10,400 --> 00:07:11,620
 you retrieve them one at a time,

169
00:07:11,620 --> 00:07:13,860
 but you've always got this long list.

170
00:07:13,860 --> 00:07:16,260
 If you have a really short site that only has like two links,

171
00:07:16,260 --> 00:07:20,620
 if you start at drchuck.com/page1.htm,

172
00:07:20,620 --> 00:07:22,420
 it'll go to page two and then go back to page one,

173
00:07:22,420 --> 00:07:23,260
 and it'll be out of things.

174
00:07:23,260 --> 00:07:25,620
 It'll have retrieved all of the pages.

175
00:07:25,620 --> 00:07:27,860
 And so if you have a website that has no external links

176
00:07:27,860 --> 00:07:30,260
 or has very few pages and they point to each other,

177
00:07:30,260 --> 00:07:32,460
 this will run out of things to do.

178
00:07:32,460 --> 00:07:37,460
 But if you go to a page like my blog or the sample stuff

179
00:07:37,460 --> 00:07:40,060
 that I have up for you to spider for testing

180
00:07:40,060 --> 00:07:43,940
 on drchuck.net, it'll run for a very long time,

181
00:07:43,940 --> 00:07:46,220
 and you'll have far more pages to retrieve

182
00:07:46,220 --> 00:07:48,340
 than pages that you retrieve, but that's okay.

183
00:07:48,340 --> 00:07:50,940
 At some point, you can stop this.

184
00:07:50,940 --> 00:07:52,680
 Maybe it stops because you ran out of bandwidth,

185
00:07:52,680 --> 00:07:55,780
 or maybe your computer went down, or who knows what, right?

186
00:07:55,780 --> 00:07:57,940
 But it's okay, this is a restartable process,

187
00:07:57,940 --> 00:08:00,340
 because it always has some pages that are retrieved

188
00:08:00,340 --> 00:08:01,740
 and some unretrieved pages.

189
00:08:01,740 --> 00:08:03,400
 You start it back up, it picks randomly

190
00:08:03,400 --> 00:08:04,860
 from the unretrieved pages.

191
00:08:04,860 --> 00:08:09,300
 The database is the sort of persistent state of your spider

192
00:08:09,300 --> 00:08:11,300
 rather than a bunch of dictionaries or lists

193
00:08:11,300 --> 00:08:14,100
 inside the Python, which go away when the program dies.

194
00:08:14,100 --> 00:08:17,820
 And so at some point, you have, let's just say,

195
00:08:17,820 --> 00:08:19,100
 a few hundred pages in here

196
00:08:19,100 --> 00:08:21,480
 and a few thousand unretrieved pages.

197
00:08:21,480 --> 00:08:23,620
 You can run the PageRank algorithm.

198
00:08:23,620 --> 00:08:25,500
 And what the PageRank algorithm does is it loops

199
00:08:25,500 --> 00:08:27,320
 through all the pages and figure out which pages

200
00:08:27,320 --> 00:08:30,160
 are linked to which pages, and then reads the numbers,

201
00:08:30,160 --> 00:08:32,760
 and then updates the numbers, and then does that

202
00:08:32,760 --> 00:08:34,660
 some number of times.

203
00:08:34,660 --> 00:08:35,940
 And so this is where the numbers,

204
00:08:35,940 --> 00:08:38,540
 all the pages sort of start out with goodness of one.

205
00:08:38,540 --> 00:08:41,700
 I think this printout is showing that goodness of one.

206
00:08:41,700 --> 00:08:45,100
 And then it changes, and then the goodness goes to,

207
00:08:45,100 --> 00:08:46,740
 the sum of the goodness goes up to two,

208
00:08:46,740 --> 00:08:49,260
 sum of the goodness goes to seven, and whatever.

209
00:08:49,260 --> 00:08:50,760
 But then it does this over and over,

210
00:08:50,760 --> 00:08:53,140
 and then it uses these numbers, and then they change again.

211
00:08:53,140 --> 00:08:55,440
 And so there's a number of time steps

212
00:08:55,440 --> 00:08:57,100
 that this PageRank runs.

213
00:08:57,100 --> 00:08:59,620
 And you will see, as the PageRank runs,

214
00:08:59,620 --> 00:09:03,260
 when I show you the code, you'll see the average

215
00:09:03,260 --> 00:09:06,980
 sort of change in these numbers across all these things,

216
00:09:06,980 --> 00:09:09,960
 and you'll see that the average goes down very rapidly

217
00:09:09,960 --> 00:09:10,800
 as you get through.

218
00:09:10,800 --> 00:09:13,640
 And so usually, with a few hundred or a thousand pages,

219
00:09:13,640 --> 00:09:16,780
 like 100 plus times you're running this algorithm,

220
00:09:16,780 --> 00:09:19,340
 and these numbers have converged.

221
00:09:19,340 --> 00:09:22,100
 And that's when you sort of can begin to trust the numbers.

222
00:09:22,100 --> 00:09:24,180
 Now there's this one program called SP Reset,

223
00:09:24,180 --> 00:09:26,820
 which sets all the pages back to one,

224
00:09:26,820 --> 00:09:28,460
 so you can start this over.

225
00:09:28,460 --> 00:09:30,640
 So if you were to spider for a while,

226
00:09:30,640 --> 00:09:32,820
 run SP Rank for a while, play around,

227
00:09:32,820 --> 00:09:34,780
 and then you wanted to spider some more,

228
00:09:34,780 --> 00:09:36,380
 and start it over, you could say,

229
00:09:36,380 --> 00:09:38,600
 oh, let's start the PageRank completely over.

230
00:09:38,600 --> 00:09:41,700
 Or you could simply take the new pages

231
00:09:41,700 --> 00:09:44,540
 and watch it adapt, either way.

232
00:09:44,540 --> 00:09:46,660
 This is just a way to reset all the pages

233
00:09:46,660 --> 00:09:50,940
 to have sort of their initial value of a goodness of 1.0.

234
00:09:50,940 --> 00:09:52,220
 So at some point, you run this.

235
00:09:52,220 --> 00:09:55,860
 This runs really, this part here runs really slow.

236
00:09:55,860 --> 00:09:58,620
 This part runs super fast, like in the blink of an eye.

237
00:09:58,620 --> 00:10:00,620
 This one is pretty fast.

238
00:10:00,620 --> 00:10:05,020
 And then at some point, you've got these pages

239
00:10:05,020 --> 00:10:07,000
 that have numbers on them.

240
00:10:07,000 --> 00:10:08,800
 They have values on the pages.

241
00:10:08,800 --> 00:10:10,400
 And there's a couple of programs

242
00:10:10,400 --> 00:10:12,420
 that allow us to visualize that.

243
00:10:12,420 --> 00:10:15,500
 One is the dump, which just reads it and checks to see.

244
00:10:15,500 --> 00:10:18,780
 It shows the new PageRank, the old PageRank,

245
00:10:18,780 --> 00:10:23,220
 and various other things, and shows just a way to dump it.

246
00:10:23,220 --> 00:10:25,700
 And then there's this thing that reads the whole thing.

247
00:10:25,700 --> 00:10:28,940
 You say, I'd like to do 25 of the best.

248
00:10:28,940 --> 00:10:31,940
 It sorts it by PageRank, and then produces a JavaScript file

249
00:10:31,940 --> 00:10:34,220
 that has just the numbers in it.

250
00:10:34,220 --> 00:10:37,680
 And then there is some HTML and a visualization library

251
00:10:37,680 --> 00:10:40,120
 called d3.js, which you can read about.

252
00:10:40,120 --> 00:10:42,340
 That when the HTML starts, it reads this

253
00:10:42,340 --> 00:10:46,060
 and has this nice force-directed layout of the PageRank.

254
00:10:46,060 --> 00:10:47,300
 And you can hover over things,

255
